 "name": "Classroom Wall Colour and Students' Emotions",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Tao, Wu, Li & Liu (2022), \"Influence of Classroom Colour Environment on College Students' Emotions during Campus Lockdown in the COVID-19 Post-Pandemic Era—A Case Study in Harbin, China\", Buildings 12:1873. Fourteen renderings (Revit 2021 + Lumion 11) of a small classroom and a large lecture hall modelled on real classrooms in Harbin form seven photo groups that vary only the wall colour: neutral, warm, warm high/low brightness, cold, cold high/low brightness. After gender, grade and major (as in Appendix A), participants observe each group and rate it on three 5-point semantic differential items (very unpleasant … very pleasant, very unrelaxed … very relaxed, very unfocused … very focused). Analysis as in the paper: means and SDs per group, repeated-measures ANOVA for hue and for brightness, t-test by gender and one-way ANOVA by grade and major. Renderings are cropped from Figure 2 of the open-access paper (CC BY 4.0) with credit; the template is removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale (14 items) is left out because it is a copyrighted instrument, so the anxiety–depression analyses cannot be repeated; (2) photo groups appear in the order of Figure 2, as the paper does not list the order used in the questionnaire; (3) the original was run on the Questionnaire Star platform during a campus lockdown in April–May 2022; (4) images are about 780 px wide from the published figure.",
